 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The method allows training on a single GPU by splitting the scene into blocks, avoiding the need for a large hash table ($2^{21}$) that would exceed GPU memory [S1, unofficial]. The hash table size is $2^{19}$ [S1, unofficial]. Training time is reduced compared to training a single large model because local models are trained on smaller subsets of data [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On the Tanks and Temples dataset, SCALAR-NeRF with hash table size $2^{19}$ outperforms Block-NeRF and Instant-NGP with hash table size $2^{21}$ in PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Scalable to large-scale scenes; avoids "foggy" artifacts common in Block-NeRF; fits on a single GPU; shared decoder promotes feature space alignment.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires KMeans clustering of camera positions; performance degrades if AABB scale factor is too large (increases hash collisions); IDW fusion is inferior to global model fusion.
-*   **When to use:** Large-scale outdoor scenes where single-GPU training is required and Block-NeRF produces foggy artifacts.
-*   **When not to use:** Small-scale scenes where a single global model suffices; scenes where blocks are very close and IDW is used (SCALAR-NeRF's global fusion is better).
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The method allows training on a single GPU by splitting the scene into blocks, avoiding the memory constraints of a single large hash table [S1, unofficial]. The hash table size is $2^{19}$, which is smaller than Block-NeRF and Instant-NGP ($2^{21}$), yet it surpasses them in performance [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Evaluated on the Tanks and Temples dataset, SCALAR-NeRF outperforms Block-NeRF, NeRF, and Instant-NGP in PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Scalability for large-scale scenes, avoidance of foggy artifacts, single-GPU training feasibility [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Performance degrades if the AABB scale factor is too large (e.g., 1.3 vs 1.2) due to hash collisions [S1, unofficial]. IDW fusion is inferior to global model fusion [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Large-scale scene reconstruction where single-GPU memory is a constraint and foggy artifacts from block blending are a concern.
+*   **When Not to Use:** Small-scale scenes where a single global model suffices, or when blocks are very close together and IDW is used (SCALAR-NeRF's global fusion mitigates this, but IDW is not recommended).
 
 ## Background
-SCALAR-NeRF was introduced by Yu Chen and Gim Hee Lee from the National University of Singapore in November 2023 [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of Block-NeRF, which suffers from foggy appearances when block centroids are close due to its inverse distance weighting fusion [S1, unofficial]. It also improves upon Instant-NGP's scalability by enabling large-scale reconstruction on a single GPU without increasing hash table size [S1, unofficial].
+SCALAR-NeRF was introduced by Yu Chen and Gim Hee Lee from the Department of Computer Science, National University of Singapore [S1, unofficial]. It was published on arXiv on November 28, 2023 [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the limitations of Block-NeRF, which produces foggy images when block centroids are close, and the memory constraints of large hash tables in Instant-NGP [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the multi-resolution hash encoding from Instant-NGP and the space contraction from Mip-NeRF360 [S1, unofficial].
 
